 {"id": "AUG-065", "title": "[P2] SCOPE: auger — decide what connecting over the muster protocol means for this service", "priority": "P2", "complexity": 2, "status": "pending", "created_at": "2026-09-24T15:16:47Z", "updated_at": "2026-09-24T15:16:47Z", "reasoning": "CONTEXT — what \"connect over muster\" means here (read before scoping).\n\nmuster (github.com/wojons/muster) turns any OpenAPI 3.x spec into a typed CLI\n(openapi-cli), an MCP server (openapi-mcp), a daemon, and a Go library. A consumer\nattaches with one config block:\n\n  {\"mcpServers\": {\"<name>\": {\"command\": \"openapi-mcp\",\n                             \"args\": [\"--spec\", \"<url-or-path>\", \"--base-url\", \"<api>\"]}}}\n\n(muster README \"mcpServers\" + docs/integration-guide.md §2; verified 2026-09-24.)\n\nDESIGN AUTHORITY for every protocol row: muster/specs/PROTOCOLS.md — \"no protocol\nimplementation row may start before its adapter satisfies this spec's pipeline and\ncross-cutting contracts on paper\". The pipeline is discover/ingest -> protocol-neutral\noperation model -> SHARED command+MCP generation -> per-protocol transport executor.\nOpenAPI 3.x is the FIRST adapter, not the engine. muster's board carries PROTO-001..008.\n\n\"Support for people to connect to us over the muster protocol\" therefore means, for this\nproject: (1) publish a muster-consumable OpenAPI 3.x contract for our own service,\n(2) ship the consumer config + a human quickstart, (3) PROVE muster's own binaries\ningest it and drive the live service. Nothing here re-implements muster — we consume it.\n\n             THIS ROW IS ONE THIRD OF A CHAIN (scope -> spec -> build) filed together\n2026-09-24 at the operator's request. Do not start this row until its predecessor\nhas landed; the spec row must not invent a surface the scope row has not decided, and\nthe build row must not guess a contract the spec row has not frozen.\n\n             MEASURED STATE OF AUGER TODAY (2026-09-24, read from the live tree):\n\n             auger has NO HTTP server at all — measured 2026-09-24: no serve verb, no http.server/HTTPServer\nbind in auger.py. It is a stdlib-only Python CLI (`python3 auger.py -n <ns> <verb>`, 13 verbs)\nthat rides DuckBrain's declared-tables HTTP API on 127.0.0.1:3000 and calls JEV for judging.\nSo auger is the one target where \"connect over muster\" has no obvious referent, and the scope\ndecision is load-bearing. The three real options:\n  (a) EXPOSE auger: add a local HTTP API over the verbs (init/ask/answer/status/dump/recall/...)\n      and publish a spec for THAT. Most work, most value — agents could drill a spec over MCP.\n  (b) EXPOSE THE SUBSTRATE: publish the DuckBrain table API contract, which auger already rides.\n      Cheap, but it exposes the database, not auger's method — probably the wrong product.\n  (c) WRAP THE CLI: ship a muster-visible wrapper so an agent drives auger's verbs as tools\n      without any HTTP surface on auger at all. Least code; weakest typing.\nRelevant prior art in-repo: AUG-052 (split question generation from judgement) and the JEV\ndependency mean any exposed surface inherits two external dependencies (DuckBrain token, JEV key)\n— the scope must say how a muster consumer authenticates to those.\n\n             Specific scope questions: choose (a), (b) or (c) with the reason recorded. If (a): which verbs are\nread-only (status/dump/recall/check/ask) versus mutating (answer/toggle/propagate/feedback/verdict) —\nthe mutating half writes decisions into DuckBrain and must be MCP-gated. Also decide whether the\nspec is generated from the argparse registry (one source of truth, matching this repo's existing\ndiscipline) or hand-written, and how auth to the two external dependencies is carried by a muster\nclient that only knows how to send HTTP headers.\n\n             DELIVERABLE — a written scope, on this row (no code):\n             1. the surface we expose and why that surface (and, explicitly, what we do NOT expose)\n       